---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import SavingsCalculator from '@components/islands/SavingsCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'savings-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How much will my savings grow?', answer: 'In "Project savings" mode, enter your starting balance, monthly deposit, expected return and time frame, and the calculator projects your future balance and how much of it is interest.' },
  { question: 'How much do I need to save each month for a goal?', answer: 'Switch to "Reach a goal" mode, enter your target amount and time frame, and the calculator solves for the monthly deposit required — accounting for your starting balance and expected growth.' },
  { question: 'How much of my income should I save?', answer: 'A common guideline is the 50/30/20 rule: roughly 50% of take-home pay for needs, 30% for wants and 20% for saving and debt repayment. Saving 20% is a solid target, but any consistent amount beats none.' },
  { question: 'How big should my emergency fund be?', answer: 'Most guidance suggests three to six months of essential expenses, kept in an accessible, low-risk account such as a high-yield savings account. Build this first, before longer-term investing, so a surprise cost does not become debt.' },
  { question: 'What return rate should I use?', answer: 'A high-yield savings account might return 3–5%, while long-term investments have historically returned more with more risk. Use a rate that matches where you will actually keep the money.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} reviewedOn="2026-07-18" intro="Project how your savings grow with regular deposits, or work backwards to find the monthly amount needed to reach a specific goal." faqs={faqs}>
  <SavingsCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Two ways to plan</h2>
    <p><strong>Project savings</strong> shows where consistent deposits will take you. <strong>Reach a goal</strong> flips the question: tell it your target and timeline, and it tells you the monthly deposit required. Both account for compound growth on your balance.</p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>Save <strong>$500 a month</strong> in an account earning <strong>4%</strong> for <strong>5 years</strong> and you end with about <strong>$33,000</strong> — roughly $30,000 of deposits plus $3,000 of interest. Push the same habit to 10 years and the interest portion grows far faster than the deposits, because each dollar has more time to compound.</p>

    <h2>How much should you save?</h2>
    <p>Two rules of thumb make a plan concrete. The <strong>50/30/20 rule</strong> puts about 20% of take-home pay toward saving and debt. And an <strong>emergency fund</strong> of three to six months of essential expenses comes first, in an accessible account, before longer-term investing — it is what keeps a surprise bill from turning into debt.</p>

    <h2>Make saving automatic</h2>
    <p>The single most effective savings habit is automation — a fixed monthly transfer you never have to think about. Small, consistent amounts compound into large balances over time, as the interest figure makes clear. To model longer-term investing, see the <a href="/finance/investment-calculator">investment calculator</a>.</p>
    <p>This is general educational information, not financial advice.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
